{"type":"message","id":"498464c3","parentId":"46399a04","timestamp":"2026-05-22T08:32:23.727Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_mpL9Do2EJjyl2chX5Y4xC99n","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"{\n  \"topic_theme\": \"Canada Proposes Crypto ATM Ban\",\n  \"primary_keyword\": \"Canada crypto ATM ban\",\n  \"primary_headline\": \"Canada Proposes Crypto ATM Ban to Tackle Scams, Money Laundering\",\n  \"sources_used\": [\"Cointelegraph\"],\n  \"source_urls\": [\"https://cointelegraph.com/news/canada-proposes-crypto-atm-ban-over-scams-and-money-laundering\"],\n  \"combined_key_facts\": [\"The Canadian government has proposed banning Bitcoin and other crypto ATMs.\", \"The government argues the machines are a primary method for scammers and money launderers.\", \"Canada was the site of the worlds first Bitcoin ATM in 2013.\", \"Canada accounts for 10.1% of global crypto ATMs, second only to the United States.\"],\n  \"aggregated_raw_content\": \"\nTitle: Canada Proposes Crypto ATM Ban to Tackle Scams, Money Laundering\n\nURL Source: https://cointelegraph.com/news/canada-proposes-crypto-atm-ban-over-scams-and-money-laundering\n\nPublished Time: Wed, 29 Apr 2026 12:15:41 GMT\n\nMarkdown Content:\nThe Canadian government has proposed banning Bitcoin and other crypto ATMs, arguing the machines have become a primary on-ramp for fraudsters and money launderers rather than a convenient access point for everyday users.\n\nThe government's Spring Economic Update 2026, published on April 28, [says](https://budget.canada.ca/update-miseajour/2026/report-rapport/chap2-en.html#a27) crypto ATMs are a “primary method for scammers to defraud victims and for criminals to place their cash proceeds of crime,” and explicitly states that the government “proposes to ban crypto ATMs.”\n\nThe proposal states that Canadians will still be able to buy virtual currencies from brick-and-mortar money services businesses, but the standalone kiosks that have proliferated in malls, gas stations and corner stores would be phased out.\n\nThe move adds to a broader push by Ottawa to clamp down on what it frames as retail-facing crypto risks as fraud cases surge, while bringing more of the digital asset sector under tighter federal oversight. Authorities say the move is aimed at cutting off one of the most common channels used in scams that have increasingly targeted Canadians.\n\nThe policy is of particular note given Canada’s early role in the sector. The world’s first publicly available Bitcoin ATM went [live](https://www.cbc.ca/news/science/world-s-first-bitcoin-atm-opens-in-vancouver-1.2286877) in a Vancouver coffee shop in 2013, making Canada the birthplace of the Bitcoin ATM.\n\n![Image 1](https://resizer.cointelegraph.com/cdn-cgi/image/f=auto,onerror=redirect,w=896,q=90/https://payload.cointelegraph.com/api/media/file/Canada.png?prefix=media%2Fcontent)\n_Spring Economic Update 2026. Source:_[_Government of Canada_](https://budget.canada.ca/update-miseajour/2026/report-rapport/chap2-en.html#a27)\n\nSince then, the country has grown into one of the most crypto-ATM-dense markets globally, a status regulators say has given it disproportionate exposure to fraud. Coin ATM Radar [data](https://coinatmradar.com/charts/geo-distribution/) estimates that Canada accounts for 10.1% of global crypto ATMs, second only to the United States.\n\nA months-long CBC investigation and internal Financial Transactions and Reports Analysis Centre of Canada (FINTRAC) analysis posted April 28 [found](https://www.cbc.ca/news/canada/toronto/canada-crypto-atm-ban-scammers-9.7180642) that crypto ATMs have become the principal method used by domestic and foreign criminal fraudsters to extract money from Canadian scam victims and push those funds into the crypto ecosystem.\n\nLaw enforcement agencies told CBC they have seen a clear uptick in cases where victims are instructed to feed cash into these machines under the guise of paying tax debts, securing romance relationships or recovering hacked accounts.\n\n_**Related:**_[_**Canada revokes 47 crypto money licenses, vows to continue**_](https://cointelegraph.com/news/canada-crypto-msb-crackdown-50-registrations-revoked)\n\n## Ban forms part of broader crypto regulatory push\n\nThe proposed ATM ban sits within a broader [effort to tighten controls](https://cointelegraph.com/features/crypto-canada-core-financial-risk-concerns-remain) around high-risk corners of Canada’s crypto market while drawing core infrastructure more firmly into the regulatory perimeter.\n\n![Image 2](https://resizer.cointelegraph.com/cdn-cgi/image/f=auto,onerror=redirect,w=896,q=90/https://payload.cointelegraph.com/api/media/file/crypto-atm-distribution.png?prefix=media%2Fcontent)\n_Crypto ATM distribution by continents and countries. Source:_[_Coin ATM Radar_](https://coinatmradar.com/charts/geo-distribution/)\n\nThe same Spring Economic Update bolsters a new Financial Crimes Agency and gives FINTRAC more tools to refuse or revoke registrations for non-compliant money services businesses, including crypto companies.\n\nIn parallel, Ottawa has [enacted](https://www.canada.ca/en/department-finance/programs/financial-sector-policy/canadas-stablecoin-framework.html) a federal stablecoin framework in Bill C-15 that makes the Bank of Canada the supervisor and requires fiat-referenced issuers to register, fully back reserves and redeem at par, with most rules kicking in after regulations are finalized ahead of an expected 2027 start date.\n\nLawmakers are also advancing Bill C-25 to [bar cryptocurrency donations in federal politics](https://cointelegraph.com/news/canada-advances-bill-ban-crypto-political-donations) over concerns about traceability and foreign interference, as the country adopts a regulation-first approach to target retail-facing abuse risks and pull core digital asset rails under federal oversight.\n\n_**Magazine:**_[_**Bitcoin will not hit $1M by 2030, says veteran trader Peter Brandt**_](https://cointelegraph-magazine.com/bitcoin-price-million-dollars-2030-prediction-trader-peter-brandt/)\n\nCointelegraph is committed to independent, transparent journalism. This news article is produced in accordance with Cointelegraph’s [Editorial Policy](https://cointelegraph.com/editorial-policy) and aims to provide accurate and timely information. Readers are encouraged to verify information independently.\n\"\n}\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1779438743726}}
{"type":"message","id":"3ed63447","parentId":"498464c3","timestamp":"2026-05-22T08:32:23.728Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_kehEQt73q8RE8UawaWao7ij2","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"{\n  \"topic_theme\": \"U.S. Senate Bans Itself from Using Prediction Markets\",\n  \"primary_keyword\": \"Senate Custom Prediction Market Ban\",\n  \"primary_headline\": \"US Senate Bans Members, Staff from Prediction Markets\",\n  \"sources_used\": [\n    \"CoinDesk\",\n    \"CoinTelegraph\",\n    \"Decrypt\"\n  ],\n  \"source_urls\": [\n    \"https://www.coindesk.com/policy/2026/04/30/u-s-senators-won-t-be-weighing-in-on-prediction-markets-bets-after-banning-themselves\",\n    \"https://cointelegraph.com/news/us-senate-bans-itself-from-prediction-markets\",\n    \"https://decrypt.co/366243/senate-votes-ban-senators-staff-using-prediction-markets\"\n  ],\n  \"combined_key_facts\": [\n    \"The US Senate unanimously approved a rule-change resolution banning senators and their staff from placing bets on prediction markets.\",\n    \"Republican Senator Bernie Moreno of Ohio authored/introduced the 14-line resolution.\",\n    \"Moreno stated that engaging in prediction markets could risk monetizing the job using inside information, deteriorating constituent confidence.\",\n    \"Senate Democratic leader Chuck Schumer threw support behind the move, calling it a 'no-brainer' preventing Congress from turning into a casino.\",\n    \"The resolution came in the wake of controversies, including a special forces soldier charged with using classified info to make Polymarket bets.\",\n    \"Republican Rep. Ashley Hinson intends to introduce a similar resolution for the House.\",\n    \"Polymarket and Kalshi leadership supported the resolution, asserting it aligns with their own internal bans on insider trading.\"\n  ],\n  \"aggregated_raw_content\": \"Acting on a simple, 14-line resolution pushed by Ohio Republican Senator Bernie Moreno, the Senate agreed unanimously to put a restriction between members and the increasingly popular, controversial betting platforms that have drawn scrutiny over insider-trading activity and fights over who has regulatory jurisdiction. “United States Senators have no business engaging in speculative activities like prediction markets while collecting a taxpayer-funded paycheck, period,” said Senator Moreno. The US Senate on Thursday unanimously approved a resolution banning its members and staff, who are often exposed to sensitive information, from using prediction markets. The resolution, passed by unanimous consent, changed the Senate's rules and took immediate effect. “Engaging in any way in a prediction market or trying to place bets where we might have inside information deteriorates the confidence that our constituents have in us,” Republican Senator Bernie Moreno, who introduced the resolution, said on the Senate floor. “By changing the standing rules of the Senate, what we’re doing is allowing our constituents to know, once and for all, that no member of the United States Senate, no member of the staff of the United States Senate, can ever use that inside information as a way to monetize this job whatsoever,” he added. The resolution comes after a special forces soldier involved in the plan to capture former Venezuelan President Nicolás Maduro was charged last week, on April 23, with using classified information to make bets on Polymarket, as lawmakers also air concerns over well-timed bets on the Iran war. Senate Democratic leader Chuck Schumer said on the Senate floor that “of all the issues we debate in Washington, this falls clearly in the category of a ‘no-brainer.’” “We must never allow Congress to turn into a casino where members representing the public can gamble on wars, or economic crises, or elections,” he said. “We should go further; this is a good start, but not enough,” Schumer said. “The administration and its employees must apply these very same rules too, particularly this administration, which shows such a troubling affinity to corruption and self-dealing.” Republican Representative Ashley Hinson posted to X that she would introduce a similar resolution to ban the use of prediction markets in the House. Polymarket posted on X that it fully supported the Senate resolution and its terms of service “already prohibit such conduct, but codifying this into law is a step forward for the industry.” Tarek Mansour, co-founder and CEO of rival prediction market platform Kalshi, also celebrated the resolution in a post on X, adding that it “already proactively blocks members of Congress and enforces against insider trading.”\"\n}\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1779438743726}}
